[interviewer · BRAINSTORM · 17:20] We don't have elasticity or competitor pricing. Beyond pricing, what else could the client do to reverse the margin decline? Give me a structured set of levers.

[candidate · 18:50] No elasticity data, so I'll treat any price move as needing a test rather than a chain-wide rollout. Four buckets beyond price.

First, cost of goods. Renegotiate or lock bean contracts with forward purchasing to stop riding the spot market. Consolidate suppliers across 200 stores for volume leverage. Substitute where quality permits — though as a specialty chain, bean quality is the brand, so that's limited.

Second, waste and portioning. Standardize recipes, meter the milk, track shrink by store. This is where the store-level variance I asked about would pay off, and it costs almost nothing to implement.

Third, mix. Push customers toward high-margin items — brewed coffee, retail beans, loyalty subscriptions — rather than low-margin food. Reprice individual items instead of an across-the-board increase, so price rises land where demand is least sensitive.

Fourth, overhead, which drifted 12 to 14 points. Smaller, but worth asking whether corporate scaled faster than the store base.

I'd sequence: waste and mix first since they're fast and low-risk, price second, contracts third.
